  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nScattered high clouds ahead of the trough off the west Coast\ncontinue to drift northeast over the area this morning. Patches of\nlow clouds and fog are developing over the San Diego County\ncoastal areas. Fog may become locally dense towards sunrise,\nespecially on the coastal mesas. Any low clouds and fog should\nquickly clear out by mid-morning. \n\nOne last day of excruciating heat is in store with high\ntemperatures around 5 to 10 degrees above normal. With far less\ncloud cover than yesterday and minimal cooling this morning, it\nwill be several degrees warmer than what ended up occurring\nyesterday, especially across the low deserts. The Heat Advisories\nand Excessive Heat Warnings all remain in effect through 8 PM this\nevening. Modest mid-level moisture remains for a slight chance of\nshowers and thunderstorms over the mountains this afternoon. With\nflow aloft turning more southwesterly, a few of these storms could\ndrift into the low desert.\n\nThe upper level high begins to progress eastward as an upper\ntrough pushes into the West Coast on Saturday. This will bring a\nfew degrees of cooling across the area, though unfortunately the\nhumidity will stick around. This trough will remain somewhat\nstationary through early next week as multiple short waves move\nthrough the mean flow to our north. This will continue the cooling\ntrend into at least Tuesday. By Sunday, high temperatures will\nfinally return to normal, though the humidity will keep minimum\ntemperatures around 5 degrees above normal. By Tuesday, highs will\nbe around 5 to 10 degrees below normal. Subtle drying also allows\nlow temperatures to return to near normal. \n\nFor late Tuesday into Thursday, there's around a 15% chance of \nmoistly light showers as mid and upper level moisture from what is\ncurrently Tropical Storm Karina potentially gets drawn north. How\nmuch moisture we get and any forcing that goes along with it will\nbe very sensitive to the track of the tropical cyclone as well as\nthe position of the upper trough off the West Coast. There is a \nlot of spread in the forecast precipitable water from the \nensembles, and to a greater extent the amount of rainfall we could\nget from it. It should be noted that the official NHC track \nforecast for this cyclone remains well to our south, so these \nsmall rain chances and increased surf are the only impact we are \nanticipating from this system. \n\nMinor warming is possible late in the week as high pressure tries\nto expand westward again, though the warmest ensemble solutions\nonly account for about 40% of the ensemble space at this point,\nwith the remaining 60% keeping temperatures closer to midweek\nvalues.",
